<speak>
<p>Let u be odd and greater than one, and let I contain its distinct prime divisors. Use the prime roots of unity chosen in Node four. First form B by adjoining i and these roots to the integers. Enlarge B to A by adjoining one over u. Thus powers of u are allowed denominators. For each subset S, multiply its chosen roots to obtain zeta sub S. Write c sub S, d sub S, and g sub S for the three evaluations. All belong to this one ring.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>The evaluated norm identities say that x sub S times its complex conjugate equals u squared, for any of these three values. Conjugation stays in B: each root of unity becomes its inverse. Since u is invertible in A, divide the conjugate by u squared. Multiplying this expression by x sub S gives one. This is an explicit inverse in A, more than just nonvanishing in the complex plane.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>For u equal to three, the ring is the integers with i, omega, and one third adjoined. The inverse of c of omega is its conjugate divided by nine. The same formula works for every subset and for d and g.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Now reduce A modulo one plus i. Because two equals the product of one plus i and one minus i, two becomes zero. We must still prove that one stays different from zero. The generators of B are roots of unity. Their powers give finitely many monomials spanning B over the integers. Also B sits inside the complex numbers, so it has no additive torsion. Hence B is a nonzero free abelian group of finite rank. Its quotient by twice B is nonzero.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Choose a maximal ideal of B containing two. Its residue field has characteristic two. In this field, the square of one plus i equals two i, which is zero. A field has no nonzero element with square zero, so one plus i itself is zero. The odd integer u, however, has residue one.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>We can therefore extend the map from B to A, sending one over u to one. It still maps onto the nonzero residue field and kills one plus i. It factors through A modulo one plus i. If that quotient were the zero ring, it could not map one to a nonzero one. Therefore our quotient is nonzero and has characteristic two. We do not need it to be a field.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>Finally, carry the inverse identity through reduction. The images of x sub S and its inverse still multiply to one. All our evaluated denominators remain units. We can divide by c sub S in this single quotient ring. Both two and one plus i become zero there, so we cannot divide by them. The exact polynomial quotients involving them were already formed before reduction. Keep this order of operations throughout the next comparisons.<break time="1200ms"/></p>
</speak>
